  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Load paths: follow the force', note: 'Every structure delivers its loads to the ground. Trace the path: slab → beam → column → foundation → soil. Structural design is making that path continuous, stiff and strong — a missing link is a collapse.' },
    { math: '2 · Bending is the beam\'s main event', note: 'A loaded beam bends: top face squeezes (compression), bottom stretches (tension). That is why an I-beam is shaped like an I — material concentrated at the flanges where stresses are highest, web holding them together.' },
    { math: '3 · Deflection vs strength', note: 'Two separate checks: stress (does it break? — material strength) and deflection (does it sag too much? — stiffness, E·I). A beam can be strong enough and still unusably bouncy — serviceability governs more designs than strength does.' },
    { math: '4 · Bridges: the span decides the type', note: 'Beams (short spans, ~25–80 m), trusses (triangulated members, ~100–300 m), arches (compression, the architecture lesson), suspension and cable-stayed (longest — Akashi Kaikyō 1,991 m: cables carry tension, towers compression, deck hanging between).' },
