<TopicPage slug="subjects/engineering/intermediate/forces-beams-and-bridges">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Structural engineering is <strong>directing forces to the ground through chosen paths</strong>.
    The beam is the core problem — bending creates tension and compression in the same member —
    and bridge types are the span-length answers history has converged on.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="load paths, bending, the two checks, the span ladder" widget="equationStep" config={beamCfg} />

  <h2>Lessons written in steel and concrete</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tacoma Narrows (1940):</strong> aeroelastic flutter — wind excited the deck\'s twist mode. The bridge was strong for traffic loads and failed on a load nobody had designed for; wind engineering became a required discipline (the aerodynamics of the fluid-dynamics lesson, structural edition).</li>
    <li><strong>Why old bridges stand:</strong> stone is strong in compression, weak in tension — arches keep everything compressed (the architecture lesson\'s catenary logic); Roman concrete plus compression-only geometry = two-millennia service life.</li>
    <li><strong>Trusses are triangles:</strong> pin-jointed triangles are rigid by geometry — every member is pure tension or compression, no bending. The analysis is solvable by hand: the 19th century\'s mathematical playground (railway bridges everywhere).</li>
    <li><strong>Prestressed concrete — the quiet revolution:</strong> steel tendons squeeze concrete before loading, so tension never appears: spans doubled, costs halved, the 20th century\'s infrastructure built from it (the materials lesson\'s stress–strain, engineered).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Petroski, To Engineer Is Human; the statics lesson (free-body diagrams, safety factors); Gordon, Structures: Or Why Things Don\'t Fall Down.</p>
</TopicPage>
